00108 {\cf20  MISSION STATEMENT}\par
00109 {\cf20  =================}\par
00110 {\cf20 }\par
00111 {\cf20  - Easy to use to create code-driven and data-driven tools.}\par
00112 {\cf20  - Easy to use to create ad hoc short-lived tools and long-lived, more elaborate tools.}\par
00113 {\cf20  - Easy to hack and improve.}\par
00114 {\cf20  - Minimize setup and maintenance.}\par
00115 {\cf20  - Minimize state storage on user side.}\par
00116 {\cf20  - Minimize state synchronization.}\par
00117 {\cf20  - Portable, minimize dependencies, run on target (consoles, phones, etc.).}\par
00118 {\cf20  - Efficient runtime and memory consumption.}\par
00119 {\cf20 }\par
00120 {\cf20  Designed primarily for developers and content-creators, not the typical end-user!}\par
00121 {\cf20  Some of the current weaknesses (which we aim to address in the future) includes:}\par
00122 {\cf20 }\par
00123 {\cf20  - Doesn't look fancy.}\par
00124 {\cf20  - Limited layout features, intricate layouts are typically crafted in code.}\par
00125 {\cf20 }\par
00126 {\cf20 }\par
00127 {\cf20  CONTROLS GUIDE}\par
00128 {\cf20  ==============}\par
00129 {\cf20 }\par
00130 {\cf20  - MOUSE CONTROLS}\par
00131 {\cf20    - Mouse wheel:                   Scroll vertically.}\par
00132 {\cf20    - SHIFT+Mouse wheel:             Scroll horizontally.}\par
00133 {\cf20    - Click [X]:                     Close a window, available when 'bool* p_open' is passed to ImGui::Begin().}\par
00134 {\cf20    - Click ^, Double-Click title:   Collapse window.}\par
00135 {\cf20    - Drag on corner/border:         Resize window (double-click to auto fit window to its contents).}\par
00136 {\cf20    - Drag on any empty space:       Move window (unless io.ConfigWindowsMoveFromTitleBarOnly = true).}\par
00137 {\cf20    - Left-click outside popup:      Close popup stack (right-click over underlying popup: Partially close popup stack).}\par
00138 {\cf20 }\par
00139 {\cf20  - TEXT EDITOR}\par
00140 {\cf20    - Hold SHIFT or Drag Mouse:      Select text.}\par
00141 {\cf20    - CTRL+Left/Right:               Word jump.}\par
00142 {\cf20    - CTRL+Shift+Left/Right:         Select words.}\par
00143 {\cf20    - CTRL+A or Double-Click:        Select All.}\par
00144 {\cf20    - CTRL+X, CTRL+C, CTRL+V:        Use OS clipboard.}\par
00145 {\cf20    - CTRL+Z, CTRL+Y:                Undo, Redo.}\par
00146 {\cf20    - ESCAPE:                        Revert text to its original value.}\par
00147 {\cf20    - On OSX, controls are automatically adjusted to match standard OSX text editing 2ts and behaviors.}\par
00148 {\cf20 }\par
00149 {\cf20  - KEYBOARD CONTROLS}\par
00150 {\cf20    - Basic:}\par
00151 {\cf20      - Tab, SHIFT+Tab               Cycle through text editable fields.}\par
00152 {\cf20      - CTRL+Tab, CTRL+Shift+Tab     Cycle through windows.}\par
00153 {\cf20      - CTRL+Click                   Input text into a Slider or Drag widget.}\par
00154 {\cf20    - Extended features with `io.ConfigFlags |= ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableKeyboard`:}\par
00155 {\cf20      - Tab, SHIFT+Tab:              Cycle through every items.}\par
00156 {\cf20      - Arrow keys                   Move through items using directional navigation. Tweak value.}\par
00157 {\cf20      - Arrow keys + Alt, Shift      Tweak slower, tweak faster (when using arrow keys).}\par
00158 {\cf20      - Enter                        Activate item (prefer text input when possible).}\par
00159 {\cf20      - Space                        Activate item (prefer tweaking with arrows when possible).}\par
00160 {\cf20      - Escape                       Deactivate item, leave child window, close popup.}\par
00161 {\cf20      - Page Up, Page Down           Previous page, next page.}\par
00162 {\cf20      - Home, End                    Scroll to top, scroll to bottom.}\par
00163 {\cf20      - Alt                          Toggle between scrolling layer and menu layer.}\par
00164 {\cf20      - CTRL+Tab then Ctrl+Arrows    Move window. Hold SHIFT to resize instead of moving.}\par
00165 {\cf20    - Output when ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableKeyboard set,}\par
00166 {\cf20      - io.WantCaptureKeyboard flag is set when keyboard is claimed.}\par
00167 {\cf20      - io.NavActive: true when a window is focused and it doesn't have the ImGuiWindowFlags_NoNavInputs flag set.}\par
00168 {\cf20      - io.NavVisible: true when the navigation cursor is visible (usually goes to back false when mouse is used).}\par
00169 {\cf20 }\par
00170 {\cf20  - GAMEPAD CONTROLS}\par
00171 {\cf20    - Enable with 'io.ConfigFlags |= ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableGamepad'.}\par
00172 {\cf20    - Particularly useful to use Dear ImGui on a console system (e.g. PlayStation, Switch, Xbox) without a mouse!}\par
00173 {\cf20    - Download controller mapping PNG/PSD at http://dearimgui.com/controls_sheets}\par
00174 {\cf20    - Backend support: backend needs to:}\par
00175 {\cf20       - Set 'io.BackendFlags |= ImGuiBackendFlags_HasGamepad' + call io.AddKeyEvent/AddKeyAnalogEvent() with ImGuiKey_Gamepad_XXX keys.}\par
00176 {\cf20       - For analog values (0.0f to 1.0f), backend is responsible to handling a dead-zone and rescaling inputs accordingly.}\par
00177 {\cf20         Backend code will probably need to transform your raw inputs (such as e.g. remapping your 0.2..0.9 raw input range to 0.0..1.0 imgui range, etc.).}\par
00178 {\cf20       - BEFORE 1.87, BACKENDS USED TO WRITE TO io.NavInputs[]. This is now obsolete. Please call io functions instead!}\par
00179 {\cf20    - If you need to share inputs between your game and the Dear ImGui interface, the easiest approach is to go all-or-nothing,}\par
00180 {\cf20      with a buttons combo to toggle the target. Please reach out if you think the game vs navigation input sharing could be improved.}\par
00181 {\cf20 }\par
00182 {\cf20  - REMOTE INPUTS SHARING & MOUSE EMULATION}\par
00183 {\cf20    - PS4/PS5 users: Consider emulating a mouse cursor with DualShock touch pad or a spare analog stick as a mouse-emulation fallback.}\par
00184 {\cf20    - Consoles/Tablet/Phone users: Consider using a Synergy 1.x server (on your PC) + run examples/libs/synergy/uSynergy.c (on your console/tablet/phone app)}\par
00185 {\cf20      in order to share your PC mouse/keyboard.}\par
00186 {\cf20    - See https://github.com/ocornut/imgui/wiki/Useful-Extensions#remoting for other remoting solutions.}\par
00187 {\cf20    - On a TV/console system where readability may be lower or mouse inputs may be awkward, you may want to set the ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableSetMousePos flag.}\par
00188 {\cf20      Enabling ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableSetMousePos + ImGuiBackendFlags_HasSetMousePos instructs Dear ImGui to move your mouse cursor along with navigation movements.}\par
00189 {\cf20      When enabled, the NewFrame() function may alter 'io.MousePos' and set 'io.WantSetMousePos' to notify you that it wants the mouse cursor to be moved.}\par
00190 {\cf20      When that happens your backend NEEDS to move the OS or underlying mouse cursor on the next frame. Some of the backends in examples/ do that.}\par
00191 {\cf20      (If you set the NavEnableSetMousePos flag but don't honor 'io.WantSetMousePos' properly, Dear ImGui will misbehave as it will see your mouse moving back & forth!)}\par
00192 {\cf20      (In a setup when you may not have easy control over the mouse cursor, e.g. uSynergy.c doesn't expose moving remote mouse cursor, you may want}\par
00193 {\cf20      to set a boolean to ignore your other external mouse positions until the external source is moved again.)}\par
00194 {\cf20 }\par
00195 {\cf20 }\par
00196 {\cf20  PROGRAMMER GUIDE}\par
00197 {\cf20  ================}\par
00198 {\cf20 }\par
00199 {\cf20  READ FIRST}\par
00200 {\cf20  ----------}\par
00201 {\cf20  - Remember to check the wonderful Wiki (https://github.com/ocornut/imgui/wiki)}\par
00202 {\cf20  - Your code creates the UI every frame of your application loop, if your code doesn't run the UI is gone!}\par
00203 {\cf20    The UI can be highly dynamic, there are no construction or destruction steps, less superfluous}\par
00204 {\cf20    data retention on your side, less state duplication, less state synchronization, fewer bugs.}\par
00205 {\cf20  - Call and read ImGui::ShowDemoWindow() for demo code demonstrating most features.}\par
00206 {\cf20    Or browse https://pthom.github.io/imgui_manual_online/manual/imgui_manual.html for interactive web version.}\par
00207 {\cf20  - The library is designed to be built from sources. Avoid pre-compiled binaries and packaged versions. See imconfig.h to configure your build.}\par
00208 {\cf20  - Dear ImGui is an implementation of the IMGUI paradigm (immediate-mode graphical user interface, a term coined by Casey Muratori).}\par
00209 {\cf20    You can learn about IMGUI principles at http://www.johno.se/book/imgui.html, http://mollyrocket.com/861 & more links in Wiki.}\par
00210 {\cf20  - Dear ImGui is a "single pass" rasterizing implementation of the IMGUI paradigm, aimed at ease of use and high-performances.}\par
00211 {\cf20    For every application frame, your UI code will be called only once. This is in contrast to e.g. Unity's implementation of an IMGUI,}\par
00212 {\cf20    where the UI code is called multiple times ("multiple passes") from a single entry point. There are pros and cons to both approaches.}\par
00213 {\cf20  - Our origin is on the top-left. In axis aligned bounding boxes, Min = top-left, Max = bottom-right.}\par
00214 {\cf20  - Please make sure you have asserts enabled (IM_ASSERT redirects to assert() by default, but can be redirected).}\par
00215 {\cf20    If you get an assert, read the messages and comments around the assert.}\par
00216 {\cf20  - This codebase aims to be highly optimized:}\par
00217 {\cf20    - A typical idle frame should never call malloc/free.}\par
00218 {\cf20    - We rely on a maximum of constant-time or O(N) algorithms. Limiting searches/scans as much as possible.}\par
00219 {\cf20    - We put particular energy in making sure performances are decent with typical "Debug" build settings as well.}\par
00220 {\cf20      Which mean we tend to avoid over-relying on "zero-cost abstraction" as they aren't zero-cost at all.}\par
00221 {\cf20  - This codebase aims to be both highly opinionated and highly flexible:}\par
00222 {\cf20    - This code works because of the things it choose to solve or not solve.}\par
00223 {\cf20    - C++: this is a pragmatic C-ish codebase: we don't use fancy C++ features, we don't include C++ headers,}\par
00224 {\cf20      and ImGui:: is a namespace. We rarely use member functions (and when we did, I am mostly regretting it now).}\par
00225 {\cf20      This is to increase compatibility, increase maintainability and facilitate use from other languages.}\par
00226 {\cf20    - C++: ImVec2/ImVec4 do not expose math operators by default, because it is expected that you use your own math types.}\par
00227 {\cf20      See FAQ "How can I use my own math types instead of ImVec2/ImVec4?" for details about setting up imconfig.h for that.}\par
00228 {\cf20      We can can optionally export math operators for ImVec2/ImVec4 using IMGUI_DEFINE_MATH_OPERATORS, which we use internally.}\par
00229 {\cf20    - C++: pay attention that ImVector<> manipulates plain-old-data and does not honor construction/destruction}\par
00230 {\cf20      (so don't use ImVector in your code or at our own risk!).}\par
00231 {\cf20    - Building: We don't use nor mandate a build system for the main library.}\par
00232 {\cf20      This is in an effort to ensure that it works in the real world aka with any esoteric build setup.}\par
00233 {\cf20      This is also because providing a build system for the main library would be of little-value.}\par
00234 {\cf20      The build problems are almost never coming from the main library but from specific backends.}\par
00235 {\cf20 }\par
00236 {\cf20 }\par
00237 {\cf20  HOW TO UPDATE TO A NEWER VERSION OF DEAR IMGUI}\par
00238 {\cf20  ----------------------------------------------}\par
00239 {\cf20  - Update submodule or copy/overwrite every file.}\par
00240 {\cf20  - About imconfig.h:}\par
00241 {\cf20    - You may modify your copy of imconfig.h, in this case don't overwrite it.}\par
00242 {\cf20    - or you may locally branch to modify imconfig.h and merge/rebase latest.}\par
00243 {\cf20    - or you may '#define IMGUI_USER_CONFIG "my_config_file.h"' globally from your build system to}\par
00244 {\cf20      specify a custom path for your imconfig.h file and instead not have to modify the default one.}\par
00245 {\cf20 }\par
00246 {\cf20  - Overwrite all the sources files except for imconfig.h (if you have modified your copy of imconfig.h)}\par
00247 {\cf20  - Or maintain your own branch where you have imconfig.h modified as a top-most commit which you can regularly rebase over "master".}\par
00248 {\cf20  - You can also use '#define IMGUI_USER_CONFIG "my_config_file.h" to redirect configuration to your own file.}\par
00249 {\cf20  - Read the "API BREAKING CHANGES" section (below). This is where we list occasional API breaking changes.}\par
00250 {\cf20    If a function/type has been renamed / or marked obsolete, try to fix the name in your code before it is permanently removed}\par
00251 {\cf20    from the public API. If you have a problem with a missing function/symbols, search for its name in the code, there will}\par
00252 {\cf20    likely be a comment about it. Please report any issue to the GitHub page!}\par
00253 {\cf20  - To find out usage of old API, you can add '#define IMGUI_DISABLE_OBSOLETE_FUNCTIONS' in your configuration file.}\par
00254 {\cf20  - Try to keep your copy of Dear ImGui reasonably up to date!}\par
00255 {\cf20 }\par
00256 {\cf20 }\par
00257 {\cf20  GETTING STARTED WITH INTEGRATING DEAR IMGUI IN YOUR CODE/ENGINE}\par
00258 {\cf20  ---------------------------------------------------------------}\par
00259 {\cf20  - See https://github.com/ocornut/imgui/wiki/Getting-Started.}\par
00260 {\cf20  - Run and study the examples and demo in imgui_demo.cpp to get acquainted with the library.}\par
00261 {\cf20  - In the majority of cases you should be able to use unmodified backends files available in the backends/ folder.}\par
00262 {\cf20  - Add the Dear ImGui source files + selected backend source files to your projects or using your preferred build system.}\par
00263 {\cf20    It is recommended you build and statically link the .cpp files as part of your project and NOT as a shared library (DLL).}\par
00264 {\cf20  - You can later customize the imconfig.h file to tweak some compile-time behavior, such as integrating Dear ImGui types with your own maths types.}\par
00265 {\cf20  - When using Dear ImGui, your programming IDE is your friend: follow the declaration of variables, functions and types to find comments about them.}\par
00266 {\cf20  - Dear ImGui never touches or knows about your GPU state. The only function that knows about GPU is the draw function that you provide.}\par
00267 {\cf20    Effectively it means you can create widgets at any time in your code, regardless of considerations of being in "update" vs "render"}\par
00268 {\cf20    phases of your own application. All rendering information is stored into command-lists that you will retrieve after calling ImGui::Render().}\par
00269 {\cf20  - Refer to the backends and demo applications in the examples/ folder for instruction on how to setup your code.}\par
00270 {\cf20  - If you are running over a standard OS with a common graphics API, you should be able to use unmodified imgui_impl_*** files from the examples/ folder.}\par
00271 {\cf20 }\par
00272 {\cf20 }\par
00273 {\cf20  HOW A SIMPLE APPLICATION MAY LOOK LIKE}\par
00274 {\cf20  --------------------------------------}\par
00275 {\cf20  EXHIBIT 1: USING THE EXAMPLE BACKENDS (= imgui_impl_XXX.cpp files from the backends/ folder).}\par
00276 {\cf20  The sub-folders in examples/ contain examples applications following this structure.}\par
00277 {\cf20 }\par
00278 {\cf20      // Application init: create a dear imgui context, setup some options, load fonts}\par
00279 {\cf20      ImGui::CreateContext();}\par
00280 {\cf20      ImGuiIO& io = ImGui::GetIO();}\par
00281 {\cf20      // TODO: Set optional io.ConfigFlags values, e.g. 'io.ConfigFlags |= ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableKeyboard' to enable keyboard controls.}\par
00282 {\cf20      // TODO: Fill optional fields of the io structure later.}\par
00283 {\cf20      // TODO: Load TTF/OTF fonts if you don't want to use the default font.}\par
00284 {\cf20 }\par
00285 {\cf20      // Initialize helper Platform and Renderer backends (here we are using imgui_impl_win32.cpp and imgui_impl_dx11.cpp)}\par
00286 {\cf20      ImGui_ImplWin32_Init(hwnd);}\par
00287 {\cf20      ImGui_ImplDX11_Init(g_pd3dDevice, g_pd3dDeviceContext);}\par
00288 {\cf20 }\par
00289 {\cf20      // Application main loop}\par
00290 {\cf20      while (true)}\par
00291 {\cf20      \{}\par
00292 {\cf20          // Feed inputs to dear imgui, start new frame}\par
00293 {\cf20          ImGui_ImplDX11_NewFrame();}\par
00294 {\cf20          ImGui_ImplWin32_NewFrame();}\par
00295 {\cf20          ImGui::NewFrame();}\par
00296 {\cf20 }\par
00297 {\cf20          // Any application code here}\par
00298 {\cf20          ImGui::Text("Hello, world!");}\par
00299 {\cf20 }\par
00300 {\cf20          // Render dear imgui into screen}\par
00301 {\cf20          ImGui::Render();}\par
00302 {\cf20          ImGui_ImplDX11_RenderDrawData(ImGui::GetDrawData());}\par
00303 {\cf20          g_pSwapChain->Present(1, 0);}\par
00304 {\cf20      \}}\par
00305 {\cf20 }\par
00306 {\cf20      // Shutdown}\par
00307 {\cf20      ImGui_ImplDX11_Shutdown();}\par
00308 {\cf20      ImGui_ImplWin32_Shutdown();}\par
00309 {\cf20      ImGui::DestroyContext();}\par
00310 {\cf20 }\par
00311 {\cf20  EXHIBIT 2: IMPLEMENTING CUSTOM BACKEND / CUSTOM ENGINE}\par
00312 {\cf20 }\par
00313 {\cf20      // Application init: create a dear imgui context, setup some options, load fonts}\par
00314 {\cf20      ImGui::CreateContext();}\par
00315 {\cf20      ImGuiIO& io = ImGui::GetIO();}\par
00316 {\cf20      // TODO: Set optional io.ConfigFlags values, e.g. 'io.ConfigFlags |= ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableKeyboard' to enable keyboard controls.}\par
00317 {\cf20      // TODO: Fill optional fields of the io structure later.}\par
00318 {\cf20      // TODO: Load TTF/OTF fonts if you don't want to use the default font.}\par
00319 {\cf20 }\par
00320 {\cf20      // Build and load the texture atlas into a texture}\par
00321 {\cf20      // (In the examples/ app this is usually done within the ImGui_ImplXXX_Init() function from one of the demo Renderer)}\par
00322 {\cf20      int width, height;}\par
00323 {\cf20      unsigned char* pixels = nullptr;}\par
00324 {\cf20      io.Fonts->GetTexDataAsRGBA32(&pixels, &width, &height);}\par
00325 {\cf20 }\par
00326 {\cf20      // At this point you've got the texture data and you need to upload that to your graphic system:}\par
00327 {\cf20      // After we have created the texture, store its pointer/identifier (_in whichever format your engine uses_) in 'io.Fonts->TexID'.}\par
00328 {\cf20      // This will be passed back to your via the renderer. Basically ImTextureID == void*. Read FAQ for details about ImTextureID.}\par
00329 {\cf20      MyTexture* texture = MyEngine::CreateTextureFromMemoryPixels(pixels, width, height, TEXTURE_TYPE_RGBA32)}\par
00330 {\cf20      io.Fonts->SetTexID((void*)texture);}\par
00331 {\cf20 }\par
00332 {\cf20      // Application main loop}\par
00333 {\cf20      while (true)}\par
00334 {\cf20      \{}\par
00335 {\cf20         // Setup low-level inputs, e.g. on Win32: calling GetKeyboardState(), or write to those fields from your Windows message handlers, etc.}\par
00336 {\cf20         // (In the examples/ app this is usually done within the ImGui_ImplXXX_NewFrame() function from one of the demo Platform Backends)}\par
00337 {\cf20         io.DeltaTime = 1.0f/60.0f;              // set the time elapsed since the previous frame (in seconds)}\par
00338 {\cf20         io.DisplaySize.x = 1920.0f;             // set the current display width}\par
00339 {\cf20         io.DisplaySize.y = 1280.0f;             // set the current display height here}\par
00340 {\cf20         io.AddMousePosEvent(mouse_x, mouse_y);  // update mouse position}\par
00341 {\cf20         io.AddMouseButtonEvent(0, mouse_b[0]);  // update mouse button states}\par
00342 {\cf20         io.AddMouseButtonEvent(1, mouse_b[1]);  // update mouse button states}\par
00343 {\cf20 }\par
00344 {\cf20         // Call NewFrame(), after this point you can use ImGui::* functions anytime}\par
00345 {\cf20         // (So you want to try calling NewFrame() as early as you can in your main loop to be able to use Dear ImGui everywhere)}\par
00346 {\cf20         ImGui::NewFrame();}\par
00347 {\cf20 }\par
00348 {\cf20         // Most of your application code here}\par
00349 {\cf20         ImGui::Text("Hello, world!");}\par
00350 {\cf20         MyGameUpdate(); // may use any Dear ImGui functions, e.g. ImGui::Begin("My window"); ImGui::Text("Hello, world!"); ImGui::End();}\par
00351 {\cf20         MyGameRender(); // may use any Dear ImGui functions as well!}\par
00352 {\cf20 }\par
00353 {\cf20         // Render dear imgui, swap buffers}\par
00354 {\cf20         // (You want to try calling EndFrame/Render as late as you can, to be able to use Dear ImGui in your own game rendering code)}\par
00355 {\cf20         ImGui::EndFrame();}\par
00356 {\cf20         ImGui::Render();}\par
00357 {\cf20         ImDrawData* draw_data = ImGui::GetDrawData();}\par
00358 {\cf20         MyImGuiRenderFunction(draw_data);}\par
00359 {\cf20         SwapBuffers();}\par
00360 {\cf20      \}}\par
00361 {\cf20 }\par
00362 {\cf20      // Shutdown}\par
00363 {\cf20      ImGui::DestroyContext();}\par
00364 {\cf20 }\par
00365 {\cf20  To decide whether to dispatch mouse/keyboard inputs to Dear ImGui to the rest of your application,}\par
00366 {\cf20  you should read the 'io.WantCaptureMouse', 'io.WantCaptureKeyboard' and 'io.WantTextInput' flags!}\par
00367 {\cf20  Please read the FAQ entry "How can I tell whether to dispatch mouse/keyboard to Dear ImGui or my application?" about this.}\par
00368 {\cf20 }\par
00369 {\cf20 }\par
00370 {\cf20  HOW A SIMPLE RENDERING FUNCTION MAY LOOK LIKE}\par
00371 {\cf20  ---------------------------------------------}\par
00372 {\cf20  The backends in impl_impl_XXX.cpp files contain many working implementations of a rendering function.}\par
00373 {\cf20 }\par
00374 {\cf20     void MyImGuiRenderFunction(ImDrawData* draw_data)}\par
00375 {\cf20     \{}\par
00376 {\cf20        // TODO: Setup render state: alpha-blending enabled, no face culling, no depth testing, scissor enabled}\par
00377 {\cf20        // TODO: Setup texture sampling state: sample with bilinear filtering (NOT point/nearest filtering). Use 'io.Fonts->Flags |= ImFontAtlasFlags_NoBakedLines;' to allow point/nearest filtering.}\par
00378 {\cf20        // TODO: Setup viewport covering draw_data->DisplayPos to draw_data->DisplayPos + draw_data->DisplaySize}\par
00379 {\cf20        // TODO: Setup orthographic projection matrix cover draw_data->DisplayPos to draw_data->DisplayPos + draw_data->DisplaySize}\par
00380 {\cf20        // TODO: Setup shader: vertex \{ float2 pos, float2 uv, u32 color \}, fragment shader sample color from 1 texture, multiply by vertex color.}\par
00381 {\cf20        ImVec2 clip_off = draw_data->DisplayPos;}\par
00382 {\cf20        for (int n = 0; n < draw_data->CmdListsCount; n++)}\par
00383 {\cf20        \{}\par
00384 {\cf20           const ImDrawList* cmd_list = draw_data->CmdLists[n];}\par
00385 {\cf20           const ImDrawVert* vtx_buffer = cmd_list->VtxBuffer.Data;  // vertex buffer generated by Dear ImGui}\par
00386 {\cf20           const ImDrawIdx* idx_buffer = cmd_list->IdxBuffer.Data;   // index buffer generated by Dear ImGui}\par
00387 {\cf20           for (int cmd_i = 0; cmd_i < cmd_list->CmdBuffer.Size; cmd_i++)}\par
00388 {\cf20           \{}\par
00389 {\cf20              const ImDrawCmd* pcmd = &cmd_list->CmdBuffer[cmd_i];}\par
00390 {\cf20              if (pcmd->UserCallback)}\par
00391 {\cf20              \{}\par
00392 {\cf20                  pcmd->UserCallback(cmd_list, pcmd);}\par
00393 {\cf20              \}}\par
00394 {\cf20              else}\par
00395 {\cf20              \{}\par
00396 {\cf20                  // Project scissor/clipping rectangles into framebuffer space}\par
00397 {\cf20                  ImVec2 clip_min(pcmd->ClipRect.x - clip_off.x, pcmd->ClipRect.y - clip_off.y);}\par
00398 {\cf20                  ImVec2 clip_max(pcmd->ClipRect.z - clip_off.x, pcmd->ClipRect.w - clip_off.y);}\par
00399 {\cf20                  if (clip_max.x <= clip_min.x || clip_max.y <= clip_min.y)}\par
00400 {\cf20                      continue;}\par
00401 {\cf20 }\par
00402 {\cf20                  // We are using scissoring to clip some objects. All low-level graphics API should support it.}\par
00403 {\cf20                  // - If your engine doesn't support scissoring yet, you may ignore this at first. You will get some small glitches}\par
00404 {\cf20                  //   (some elements visible outside their bounds) but you can fix that once everything else works!}\par
00405 {\cf20                  // - Clipping coordinates are provided in imgui coordinates space:}\par
00406 {\cf20                  //   - For a given viewport, draw_data->DisplayPos == viewport->Pos and draw_data->DisplaySize == viewport->Size}\par
00407 {\cf20                  //   - In a single viewport application, draw_data->DisplayPos == (0,0) and draw_data->DisplaySize == io.DisplaySize, but always use GetMainViewport()->Pos/Size instead of hardcoding those values.}\par
00408 {\cf20                  //   - In the interest of supporting multi-viewport applications (see 'docking' branch on github),}\par
00409 {\cf20                  //     always subtract draw_data->DisplayPos from clipping bounds to convert them to your viewport space.}\par
00410 {\cf20                  // - Note that pcmd->ClipRect contains Min+Max bounds. Some graphics API may use Min+Max, other may use Min+Size (size being Max-Min)}\par
00411 {\cf20                  MyEngineSetScissor(clip_min.x, clip_min.y, clip_max.x, clip_max.y);}\par
00412 {\cf20 }\par
00413 {\cf20                  // The texture for the draw call is specified by pcmd->GetTexID().}\par
00414 {\cf20                  // The vast majority of draw calls will use the Dear ImGui texture atlas, which value you have set yourself during initialization.}\par
00415 {\cf20                  MyEngineBindTexture((MyTexture*)pcmd->GetTexID());}\par
00416 {\cf20 }\par
00417 {\cf20                  // Render 'pcmd->ElemCount/3' indexed triangles.}\par
00418 {\cf20                  // By default the indices ImDrawIdx are 16-bit, you can change them to 32-bit in imconfig.h if your engine doesn't support 16-bit indices.}\par
00419 {\cf20                  MyEngineDrawIndexedTriangles(pcmd->ElemCount, sizeof(ImDrawIdx) == 2 ? GL_UNSIGNED_SHORT : GL_UNSIGNED_INT, idx_buffer + pcmd->IdxOffset, vtx_buffer, pcmd->VtxOffset);}\par
00420 {\cf20              \}}\par
00421 {\cf20           \}}\par
00422 {\cf20        \}}\par
00423 {\cf20     \}}\par
00424 {\cf20 }\par
00425 {\cf20 }\par
00426 {\cf20  API BREAKING CHANGES}\par
00427 {\cf20  ====================}\par
00428 {\cf20 }\par
00429 {\cf20  Occasionally introducing changes that are breaking the API. We try to make the breakage minor and easy to fix.}\par
00430 {\cf20  Below is a change-log of API breaking changes only. If you are using one of the functions listed, expect to have to fix some code.}\par
00431 {\cf20  When you are not sure about an old symbol or function name, try using the Search/Find function of your IDE to look for comments or references in all imgui files.}\par
00432 {\cf20  You can read releases logs https://github.com/ocornut/imgui/releases for more details.}\par
00433 {\cf20 }\par
00434 {\cf20 (Docking/Viewport Branch)}\par
00435 {\cf20  - 2024/XX/XX (1.XXXX) - when multi-viewports are enabled, all positions will be in your natural OS coordinates space. It means that:}\par
00436 {\cf20                           - reference to hard-coded positions such as in SetNextWindowPos(ImVec2(0,0)) are probably not what you want anymore.}\par
00437 {\cf20                             you may use GetMainViewport()->Pos to offset hard-coded positions, e.g. SetNextWindowPos(GetMainViewport()->Pos)}\par
00438 {\cf20                           - likewise io.MousePos and GetMousePos() will use OS coordinates.}\par
00439 {\cf20                             If you query mouse positions to interact with non-imgui coordinates you will need to offset them, e.g. subtract GetWindowViewport()->Pos.}\par
00440 {\cf20 }\par
00441 {\cf20  - 2024/07/25 (1.91.0) - obsoleted GetContentRegionMax(), GetWindowContentRegionMin() and GetWindowContentRegionMax(). (see #7838 on GitHub for more info)}\par
00442 {\cf20                          you should never need those functions. you can do everything with GetCursorScreenPos() and GetContentRegionAvail() in a more simple way.}\par
00443 {\cf20                             - instead of:  GetWindowContentRegionMax().x - GetCursorPos().x}\par
00444 {\cf20                             - you can use: GetContentRegionAvail().x}\par
00445 {\cf20                             - instead of:  GetWindowContentRegionMax().x + GetWindowPos().x}\par
00446 {\cf20                             - you can use: GetCursorScreenPos().x + GetContentRegionAvail().x // when called from left edge of window}\par
00447 {\cf20                             - instead of:  GetContentRegionMax()}\par
00448 {\cf20                             - you can use: GetContentRegionAvail() + GetCursorScreenPos() - GetWindowPos() // right edge in local coordinates}\par
00449 {\cf20                             - instead of:  GetWindowContentRegionMax().x - GetWindowContentRegionMin().x}\par
00450 {\cf20                             - you can use: GetContentRegionAvail() // when called from left edge of window}\par
00451 {\cf20  - 2024/07/15 (1.91.0) - renamed ImGuiSelectableFlags_DontClosePopups to ImGuiSelectableFlags_NoAutoClosePopups. (#1379, #1468, #2200, #4936, #5216, #7302, #7573)}\par
